## IMMEDIATE ATTENTION REQUIRED

This bulletin summarizes a chemical exposure incident that occurred on December 2, 2025, at the Albuquerque Manufacturing Facility. All employees working with composite materials must read and acknowledge this bulletin.

---

## Incident Summary

| Element | Detail |
|---------|--------|
| Date | December 2, 2025 |
| Time | 10:45 AM MST |
| Location | Building 3, Curing Oven Area |
| Injury Type | Respiratory irritation |
| Severity | OSHA Recordable (Restricted Work) |
| Days Restricted | 5 |
| Equipment | Curing Oven #7 |

## What Happened

An employee was exposed to epoxy vapors during an unplanned venting event from Curing Oven #7. The exhaust fan had failed, but the sensor alarm was in maintenance mode and did not alert operators. The employee experienced respiratory irritation and was placed on restricted duty.

## Root Causes

| Factor | Finding |
|--------|---------|
| Equipment | Exhaust fan motor failure |
| Detection | Alarm bypassed during maintenance |
| Procedure | Work authorized without proper verification |
| Supervision | Sign-off without confirming safety systems |

## What We Found

1. **Equipment Failure:** The exhaust fan motor failed due to bearing wear. Vibration analysis had flagged this unit 3 weeks prior, but replacement was delayed.

2. **Alarm Bypass:** The alarm was placed in maintenance mode for sensor calibration but was not restored. There was no time limit or auto-restore feature.

3. **Procedural Gap:** The pre-work checklist did not require verification of alarm status before oven operation.

4. **Supervision:** The supervisor signed the work permit without physically verifying exhaust system operation.

